  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Widespread stratus, drizzle, and showers persist, \nwith new development over NW Iowa expected to continue into the \nevening hours. Temperatures with the clouds are quite cool today, \nonly reaching the mid-50s, some 10-15 degrees below normal. \n\nTONIGHT: The large upper low will slowly wobble southeast tonight, \nmost likely bringing an end to the widespread shower activity \nthrough the night. HOWEVER, soundings show an extensive low \nstratus layer persisting, with the depth of this low-lvl airmass\ncombined with weak forcing allowing drizzle and areas of lower \nvisibility to continue into daybreak Tuesday. \n\nTUESDAY:  The upper low moves away from the area with a very slow \narrival of shortwave ridging by mid-day.  Drizzle may linger into \nmid-late morning in a few areas as another weak 700mb vorticity area \nslides towards the Tri-State area mid-morning. Widespread cloud\ncover is now anticipated into early/mid afternoon. Eventually \nwe may see stratus thin and some breaks appear by the late \nafternoon, but temperatures will again remain nearly 10 degrees \nbelow normal either side of 60 degrees. \n\nWEDNESDAY-THURSDAY:  Mid-lvl heights rise as we head into Wednesday, \npartially in response to a deeper trough lifting towards the Central \nand Northern Rockies. The arrival of this trough will accomplish a \nfew things.  Firstly, inducing lee side troughing in the western \nHigh Plains and turning surface winds nearby towards the south. \nSecondly, inducing a 40 knot southerly low level jet by Wednesday \nafternoon allowing low-lvl moisture to stream back northward into \nthe central Dakotas. Finally, bringing a bit more warm air northward \nwhich should allow us to warm into the 60s in most areas with the \nlower 70s in south central South Dakota. \n\nShower and isolated thunderstorm development may develop by late \nWednesday afternoon and slide east in the evening and overnight \nhours, driven by broad warm advection, weak dPVA from the wave to \nour northwest, but also the arrival of a secondary area of vorticity \nlifting out of the Southwest US. Rainfall amounts are not expected\nto be excessive, but the 25/75th 24hr QPF from the NBM would \nsuggest amounts between 0.25 and 1.5 inches. The GEFS/EC/GEPS mean\nQPF sits around 1.1 in most locations. At this junction I'm not \nanticipating any severe weather risks, with MUCAPE around 500 J/KG\non average with the passage of this precipitation. \n\nWe'll gradually clear out during the second half of Thursday, but \nthere will be some potential for additional clouds to move in by \nFriday morning. Temperatures behind the precipitation Thursday will \nrebound back to the upper 60s to lower 70s depending on clearing.\n\nFRIDAY-SUNDAY: A wet September will continue as we approach next \nweekend with yet another progressive upper trough lifting through \nthe Rockies and into the Plains by late Friday into Saturday. This\nwave will bring high rain chances (>60% as of this product) Friday\nnight into Saturday morning. Temperatures will trend slightly warmer\nfor the upcoming weekend with most guidance settling on highs near\nnormal (upper 60s to lower 70s).",
